<!--
    The call-site stack placeholder strip.

    A call that may take stack arguments cannot find them until the caller's
    stack-pointer value at that site is known, so ActionFuncLink appends a
    placeholder input to every CALL and the rule pool later reads the offset
    back out of it. Where the site resolves, the placeholder is removed on the
    success path; where the call spec recovered its own arguments, the final
    input rewrite drops it. Neither happens here: the buffer is alloca'd, so the
    stack pointer at the call sites is not a constant offset from the frame
    base and nothing resolves, and both callees carry a declared prototype
    (strncpy, strlen are in the 28-entry base table), so the call's input list
    is locked and never rewritten. The placeholder then rendered as one
    argument past the declared arity, reading the very slot the `call` pushed
    its return address into:

        strncpy(...,a0,(int8)(a1 + -1),v4[v1 + -8]);
        strlen(v7,v4[v1 + -8]);

    Stripping it unconditionally when the stack space is heritaged is what the
    C++ Heritage::clearStackPlaceholders does. No option: the emitted call
    contradicted the prototype the same output declares, in a direction with no
    reading under which it is right.

    Only the base table is involved, so `libcsigs` does not reach this test.

    Source: decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/callplaceholder_x86_64.c
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
